## Introduction
IntroductionMany laboratories have now clearly documented the utility of selection from a large library of variants for the isolation of peptides or proteins with novel properties in a number of biological systems. The linkage of each library member with the nucleic acid encoding it provides a means of identification of rare variant species interacting with a probe molecule, particularly if coupling with a biological replicon is also provided to enable amplification of the selected unit. Although surface expression demonstrably or potentially enabling such selection processes has been demonstrated with whole bacterial cells or modified bacterial surface structures [1,2], or eukaryotic viruses [3-5], bacteriophage surface display has been the most successful and widely used [1,6]. In addition to selection for novel functions, other applications for phage surface expression are possible; an indication of the potential scope of this technology is provided in Figure 1. This review focuses on the surface display of proteins on bacteriophages, placing recent developments into the appropriate context within the field. Reference to peptide display is pertinent in some cases where the study may have additional relevance to larger proteins.
## Benefits and limitations of different phage display systems
Filamentous phage (M13/fd) display systems have used amino-terminal fusion with the gene III 'pilot protein' product or the major coat protein (the gene VIII product) [1,6], or, more recently, carboxy-terminal fusion with the gene VI product [7]. All virion proteins in filamentous phage must undergo transport into the Escherichia coli host cell periplasm prior to assembly and extrusion from
## Figure 1
the host as assembled particles, which may actually be beneficial for promoting the correct folding of certain foreign secreted proteins [8]. Other proteins, however, may be less compatible with the assembly transport process. Attempts to display certain proteins on filamentous phage may result in strong selective pressures for destabilizing mutations without necessarily destroying folding and function per se. In a study involving filamentous phage display of streptococcal protein G, O'Neil et al. [9] found that mutants that destabilize the protein (although they
allow folding and selectable function) also provided a growth advantage, as inferred from a relatively large plaque phenotype. They suggested that destabilizing mutants might promote membrane transport of the protein G-phage gene III product fusions. It is clear in principle that the inherent requirements of filamentous phage assembly act as a limiting factor for general protein display with this phage class. At the same time, it seems unlikely that host genome manipulation [10] or co-expression of chaperonins [11] could overcome all such holes in the potential filamentous phage display repertoire.
This problem was a major motivating factor for the recent development by three different groups [12-14] of display systems in the bacteriophage lambda, which has a lytic or lysogenic life cycle. As such, constraints on the surface expression of cytoplasmic proteins are theoretically removed. On the other hand, disulphide bonding and folding of naturally secreted proteins displayed on the lambda surface may be at a distinct disadvantage compared with filamentous phage [8]. Although this has not yet been formally proven, at the present time, no single phage system is likely to be compatible with all protein display applications. The existing lambda display systems use either carboxy-terminal fusion with the major tail tube protein (the product of the \(V\) gene) [12,14] or amino-terminal fusion with the product of the \(D\) gene, a head component [13]. The permissible valency of incorporation of large proteins into the lambda tail tube as V-protein fusions appears limited [12,14] but very high multiplicities of incorporation of at least some peptides are possible [15].
The surface expression of multimeric proteins may present additional challenges to any phage system. Certain functional homodimeric or homotrimeric proteins have been successfully displayed on filamentous phage [6], where association between the subunits (fused with the phage gene III product) occurs in the periplasm with at least one of the subunit fusions incorporated into a phage particle. Heterodimers such as Fab antibody fragments [16] can be displayed on such phage by co-expressing both subunits but with only one fused to the appropriate phage protein. The large tetrameric enzyme \(\beta\)-galactosidase has been displayed on the phage lambda tail tube [12,14]. One approach uses a genetic system for the partial suppression of an amber codon between the essential core of the \(V\) gene product and the foreign protein, resulting in the co-expression of the V protein core, the V protein core-foreign protein fusion, and the free foreign protein [12]. This allows phage display with only one foreign protein subunit necessarily covalently linked via the V protein into the tail structure, thus reducing potentially deleterious effects such as cross-linking between tail tubes. However, under certain conditions, expression of V protein- \(\beta\)-galactosidase alone is quite compatible with tail tube incorporation and phage growth [14]. It was suggested that a linker sequence of suitable length between the V protein and a foreign segment may be important to promote the co-incorporation of enzyme subunit fusions into the same tube structure [14].
