Recently two additional double-stranded DNA phage display systems have been described, using T4 (carboxy-terminal fusion with the fibritin gene) [17] or P4 (amino-terminal fusion with the \(P_{\mathsf{S}\mathsf{v}}\) gene) [18]. It is not yet clear whether these systems will offer special advantages over previously existing technologies.
## Selection of proteins from phage surface libraries
## Engineered libraries
This field includes any surface-expressed libraries whose diversity has been generated by an artificial process. The compatibility of a functional protein or protein domain with surface display is an obvious necessary precondition to library production. In addition to previously noted examples [6], hen egg-white lysozyme [19], human urokinase plasminogen activator epidermal growth factor-like domain [20] and chicken cystatin [21] have now been displayed on filamentous phage as amino-terminal fusions with the gene III product. Recent work with surface-displayed diverse structural libraries is considered below.
## Rational protein design versus selection for function
Selection from a phage-displayed random library of protein variants does not, in principle, require any structural information regarding the parent protein of interest. However, the numbers of clones that must be screened to achieve complete representation of all possible mutants rapidly becomes prohibitive even for the display technologies, particularly if multiple mutations are present in each library member [6]. For this reason, knowledge of the three-dimensional structure of a protein of interest can facilitate the efficacy of display selection by enabling the rational choice of residues for randomization at or near an active site or important functional region. This principle of directed randomization combined with filamentous phage display has been further exploited recently with selection of the following: novel glutathione transferases with altered specificities [22]; variants of the serine protease inhibitor ectin with high affinities for urokinase-type plasminogen activator [23]; human interleukin-6 variants with elevated receptor affinity [24]; and variants of ciliary neurotrophic factor with increased affinity for a target receptor protein [25].
A number of groups have previously demonstrated that the design of zinc finger DNA binding proteins is a fruitful and important application of directed randomization and phage display [26]. The principles and knowledge gained from this approach have great promise for the design and intracellular use of transcriptionally active sequence-specific DNA-binding proteins [27].
Although it is clear that specific targeting of sites for mutagenesis increases the prospects for covering the set of all possible mutants, random mutations derived from error-prone PCR over a extended coding sequence can still be productive for display-based selection. In the case of staphylococcal nuclease, random mutants derived in such a manner were subjected to four rounds of filamentous phage display selection with substrate analogs, yielding a variant with improved activity on a thymidine-containing small molecular substrate [28]. The importance of a powerful selection process here is emphasized by studies with antibody \( \mathbf{V_{H}} \) genes, where the random dispersal of mutations over the whole \( \mathbf{V_{H}} \) sequence was observed to result in a high proportion of completely inactive mutant proteins [29].
In recent times, considerable effort has been directed towards the rational design of alternatives to immunoglobulin-derived proteins for the selection of novel binding molecules from randomized display libraries [30]. A number of groups have investigated such alternative molecular scaffolds or frameworks within the past year [31,32,33,34,35,36]. Loop regions within small framework polypeptides of varying structures are often the targets for randomization. The molecular nature of the frameworks themselves include a zinc-finger motif [31], the Kunitz domain of the Alzheimer's amyloid \( \pmb{\uprho} \)-protein precursor [32], a four-helix bundle protein [33], an \( \pmb{\upalpha} \)-helical domain from staphylococcal protein A [34] and small \( \pmb{\upbeta} \)-proteins [35,36]. Randomization and phage display of two loops within cytochrome \( b_{562} \) (a four-helix bundle protein) allowed the selection of variants with selective binding towards a bovine serum albumin-ligand complex [33]. However, the binding affinities of the cytochrome mutants were low relative to a monoclonal antibody against the same ligand. It was suggested that the variable loops within the cytochrome structural library may be incapable of providing an efficient binding cavity for a small ligand, in contradistinction to an antibody combining site [33]. This may be compared with the superior affinity improvements obtained in a different framework molecule directed against a protein target [35]. Further investigation of the potential of this framework \( \pmb{\upbeta} \)-protein (the 'minibody') resulted in the refinement of specificity and affinity of a previously isolated minibody interleukin-6 antagonist [35].
Rational design and functional selection processes can be combined in other ways. At least with a small polypeptide structure, the deleterious effects of rationally introduced changes can be rectified by the randomization of noncritical residues and selection from phage display libraries, ultimately leading to a significantly improved molecule [37]. Once obtained by phage display selection, a novel or improved functional region can subsequently be grafted into a new protein context in a rational manner, with the aim of generating molecules with hybrid activities [38].
## Antibodies and related molecules
Immunological applications of phage display are among the most important and widely used [16]. For the purposes of this review, we will consider applications involving the display and selection of antibody-related molecules, rather than the converse situation of epitope mapping with a phage random peptide library. Continued refinements were made in antibody-affinity maturation or binding analysis by phage display combined with in vitro generation of diversity by chain shuffling [39,40] or complementarity-determining region (CDR) random mutagenesis [29,41,42-45,46]. The CDR walking mutagenesis strategy using sequential mutagenesis and selection of individual CDRs [46] generated Fab fragments against the HIV-1 gp120 with very high affinities. Most combinations of individually optimized CDRs here showed unpredictable and complex additivity, giving, at best, modest affinity improvements. It was concluded [46] that CDR additivity effects were difficult to rationalize in terms of known antibody structures and were probably due to direct interactions, although long-range effects could not be excluded. Another group [43] found that the combination of two individually selected mutations within the third CDR of the heavy chain of a Fab against IL- \( \cdot\upbeta \) resulted in a 10-fold increase in affinity. The randomization and display-selection process itself failed to produce this mutant directly, evidently through its relatively lower affinity for immobilized over soluble target protein [43]. Maturation studies aim to generate combining sites with maximal affinity, often with therapeutic applications in mind. However, a cautionary note has been sounded [41] to the effect that affinity maximization may exact a cost in antibody specificity. In an antihapten system, the tuning of affinity against cognate antigen at two critical positions in the Fab heavy chain CDR2 elicited concomitant significant affinity for denatured DNA [41]. It was pointed out that the natural immune system is very likely to possess mechanisms for selecting against such potentially deleterious 'dual-specific' mutants, which, of course, is not the case for in vitro affinity selection by phage display.
On the other hand, phage display selection can be used to overcome some inherent biases in the immune system. In the past, it has been difficult to generate monoclonal antibodies with the same specificity against peptides in the context of MHC molecules, owing to the differing maturation processes for T and B cells [47]. However, Fab fragments with affinities in the nanomolar range against a specific peptide-MHC complex could be isolated from an immunized library by filamentous phage display [47]. Interactions involving the T-cell receptor \( \pmb{\upalpha} \) chain itself have now been studied by phage display [48]. In this system, multivalent display using the filamentous phage major coat protein was chosen to enhance avidity for the selection of binding interaction with relatively low affinity.
Other recent advances involve structural aspects of displayed antibody combining regions or the means for generation of high library diversity. From comparison with camelid antibody heavy chains that lack accompanying light chains, human \(\mathrm{V_{H}}\) domains can be modified to increase solubility [49] and displayed on phage. After directed randomization and selection, specific modified \(\mathrm{V_{H}}\) domains were obtained with desired specificities [49]. The innovative use of the Cre/lox recombinational system as a generator of diversity by antibody chain shuffling has been previously discussed [30]. A recent modification of this principle involves the introduction of the recombinational signals into a \(\mathrm{V_{H}}\) gene coding sequence [50]. The application of in vitro DNA shuffling towards antibody display library diversification also has promise for further extending the range of such libraries and their utility for in vitro selection [51,52].
## Protein structural studies
Where the choice of a region of protein coding sequence for randomization is based on prediction rather than a definitive known structure, phage display selection may yield structural supporting evidence as well as generating potentially useful variants [25]. To study interactions between plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 (PAI-1) and thrombin or tissue-type plasminogen activator, Van Meijer et al. [53] used filamentous display libraries of PAI-1 randomly mutagenized over the whole protein coding sequence. Competitive selection techniques were then employed to isolate and analyze binding mutants, enabling identification of residues of critical importance in PAI-1 interactions.
Protein folding can also be productively studied by phage display. With filamentous display of the B1 domain of streptococcal protein G on the gene III product, O'Neil et al. [9] found an apparent correlation between large-plaque morphology and destabilizing mutations in the B1 domain that still allowed folding and selection by binding to IgG. This proved valuable for the identification and analysis of a panel of destabilized yet folded and functional mutants. Another group used phage display for the analysis of peptostreptococcal protein L [54]. They did not find comparable effects on phage production but attributed this to their use of a phagemid system producing a protein L-gene VIII product fusion, where only limited incorporation of the fusion protein occurs per assembled phage particle. However, based on the validated premise that only folded protein L can bind IgG, it was possible to functionally select a range of mutants retaining folding, most of which were destabilized relative to wild-type protein [54]. Such analyses [53,54] indicate the possibility of accumulating large databases pertaining to the sequence requirements for specific protein folding cases, with the likelihood of thereby deriving more generalizable information.
## cDNA libraries
In the most general terms, the total set of phage-displayed libraries of variant proteins can include the diversity of structures present within any expressed cDNA library (Fig. 1). Selection from such cDNA libraries displayed on phage surfaces can be performed in principle by searching for interactions with any desired protein or ligand. Randomly fragmented prokaryotic genomic libraries lacking introns [55] are functionally similar in this regard when expressed and displayed on phage. Because display systems with the gene III or VIII products of filamentous phage employ amino-terminal fusion with the phage proteins, they are unsuitable for surface expression of full-length cDNAs bearing stop codons. This problem was elegantly side-stepped by using the c-fos/c-jun leucine zipper interaction to provide a linkage between the gene III product and surface-expressed cDNAs [56,57]. However, fusion of foreign inserts at the carboxyl terminus of the filamentous phage gene VI is compatible with phage propagation, and monovalent gene VI-based cDNA surface libraries have been generated and screened [7]. Another approach exploits the ability of the filamentous phage gene III product to be functionally separated into two segments: an amino-terminal region binding the target host cell \(\mathbf{F^{\prime}}\) pilus for phage entry, and a carboxy-terminal region providing anchorage into the phage particle. Although cleavage of the gene III product into these separate functional domains is incompatible with phage propagation, infectivity can be restored by joining the segments through noncovalent protein-protein interactions. This can be effected with an interaction between a probe protein fused to the amino terminus of the gene III carboxy-terminal region and a constituent of a cDNA library fused to the carboxyl terminus of the gene III amino-terminal region, providing powerful selection for proteins within the library that interact with the probe molecule [58]. The same principle can be applied to the selection of displayed antibodies [59,60]. In its exploitation of the functional modularity of protein domains, this method ('direct interaction rescue') can be considered an analog of the two-hybrid system [61] although its relative sensitivity is not yet clear. Recently, direct interaction rescue has been applied to demonstrate interaction between the c-jun leucine zipper and the ribosomal protein L18a [62], and the HIV-1 reverse transcriptase large subunit and \(\upbeta\) -ectin [63].
All filamentous phage cDNA surface libraries will be subject to the same constraints limiting the transport and secretion of proteins in such phage assembly (as discussed above). However, despite the likelihood of significant under-representation or absence of certain expressed cDNAs from such libraries, it seems clear that they can nevertheless yield very useful results. Surface cDNA libraries in one of the existing lambda display systems may complement whatever shortcomings exist with corresponding filamentous phage libraries [8].
An expansion of the utility of phage-displayed cDNA libraries may come from their potential ability to activate surface structures in vitro such that they are rendered selectable with a protein or ligand probe. Certain target structures within a surface library will require a post-translational modification (e.g. phosphorylation) or binding to a partner protein before they effectively interact with the probe molecule. Such modifications can potentially be applied prior to selection. At least one protein kinase can efficiently and specifically phosphorylate a target sequence displayed on the lambda tail tube [14]. In the absence of specific knowledge regarding the nature of the required modification(s), in vitro activation of a target within the library might succeed by treatment with extracts from the same cellular source as used for cDNA generation, if they contain the appropriate factors in an active form.
## Other applications of phage display of proteins
In Figure 1b, another category of applications of phage display is noted. Here, unlike all of the applications within Figure 1a, in principle, phage virions are modified by surface display of desired proteins or peptides without there existing any essential link between the phage genotype and the surface phenotype. If a phage virion is surface-modified for transfection purposes then it functions as a delivery vehicle for the encapsidated nucleic acid; the modified surface proteins may consequently be encoded in trans by the host cell within which the phage particles are propagated. Modification of phage for presentation to the immune system is primarily concerned with the arrangement of foreign peptides or proteins on the virion surface.
However, these applications have not been as widely investigated, perhaps due to the large range of alternative systems being developed in each case. One group [64] have previously shown that filamentous phage displaying an integrin-binding site were bound and taken up by target mammalian cells in a specific manner, although it was conceded that single-stranded DNA phage would probably not make ideal delivery vectors. Double-stranded DNA phage, such as lambda, might be a better starting point for such development, and Sternberg and Hoess [13] have suggested the future use of capsid modifications on the lambda D protein for transfection purposes. On the basis of the success of in vitro assembly studies with the Bacillus subtilis double-stranded DNA phage \(\phi29\), others [65] have proposed the use of this system with similar mammalian cell delivery aims. A rather different phage delivery approach [66] has used physical encapsidation of a ricin A chain-RNA stem loop conjugate into phage MS2 particles. Although not a phage display process per se, it may be combined with protein display to enhance cell targeting specificity [66].
Recent continuing studies have shown that surface-expression of short segments of viral proteins on filamentous phage is effective for the generation of humoral immune responses [67,68]. In the absence of adjuvant, phage display of a segment from the V3 loop of HIV-1 gp120 resulted in the generation of virus-neutralizing antibody [68]. Co-display of target peptide or protein antigens with immunomodulatory molecules on the same virion surface may be a useful future strategy.
## Conclusions
In the past year, the use of phage display of proteins has continued to expand, in most cases consolidating and refining developments achieved in the recent past. Inherent limitations to this technology do, of course, exist. In addition to the biological constraints upon protein display in different phage systems, the diversity of all members of variant phage surface libraries is limited to combinations of the twenty natural amino acids. However, there is no such restriction on the peptide, protein or ligand used for selection from display libraries. This principle has recently been explored in an ingenious manner, by screening a conventional filamentous phage peptide library with a synthetic D-enantiomer of the Src homology domain 3. Selected L-peptides were used to produce the corresponding D-peptide enantiomers which, by dictates of symmetry, bind to the natural Src domain [69]. This approach is chiefly limited only by present D-protein synthetic capabilities.
Among the more important future achievements based on phage display, further success with the directed design of DNA-binding proteins may be expected. The importance of this theme is quite clear, especially when applied to tailor-made transcriptional control [27]. Phage display also has a pivotal role to play in the emerging development of certain protein tools for research and ultimately therapeutic uses. An important category of these are intracellular antibodies [70], which act as designed intracellular binding proteins for research or therapeutic application. The display technology is of importance here for the initial selection and affinity tuning of the desired specificity (whatever the optimal framework protein ultimately chosen), prior to its intracellular expression and targeting. Finally, it may be said that one of the future challenges to biology is the integration of accumulating knowledge into a global understanding of the complex interactional networks governing living systems. It is a reasonable expectation that the diverse and rapidly developing technologies of phage display may play a role in this, in parallel with in vivo systems [71].
